{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "PR Review",
  "description": "Adversarial code review of the current branch. Summarizes chat context, then delegates to a sub-agent that gathers the branch diff and performs a two-pass review: understand intent, then attack the code.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "The user wants an adversarial PR review of the current branch.\n\n## Your job (main agent)\n\nYou are the COORDINATOR. You do NOT run the review script yourself. You delegate to a sub-agent.\n\n### Step 1 — Mine the conversation for context\n\nScan the ENTIRE current chat session and write a concise summary (max 10-15 lines) covering:\n\n- **What we've been working on**: the feature, bugfix, or refactor\n- **Problems we solved**: errors, failures, debugging sessions\n- **Design decisions made**: WHY we chose specific approaches\n- **Things the user explicitly decided**: trade-offs, rejected alternatives\n- **Known limitations**: things we left for later, TODOs mentioned\n\nIf the conversation has no relevant context (user just triggered the hook cold), write: \"No prior context from this chat session.\"\n\n### Step 2 — Determine target branch\n\nCheck if the user specified a branch in their message. If yes, pass it. If no, the script will auto-detect main/master.\n\n### Step 3 — Delegate to sub-agent\n\nUse `invokeSubAgent` with name `general-task-execution` and this prompt structure:\n\n```\nYou are an ADVERSARIAL CODE REVIEWER. Your job is to find bugs, issues, and problems in a branch's changes.\n\n## Context from the working session\n\n<paste your summary from Step 1 here>\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Run the review context script:\n   ```bash\n   bash .kiro/scripts/pr-review-context.sh [target-branch-if-specified]\n   ```\n\n2. Read the output carefully. It contains:\n   - Branch info and commit log\n   - File stats and diffs (some truncated for large files)\n   - Review instructions\n\n3. For any truncated files that look suspicious, use readFile to read the full file and understand the context around the changes.\n\n4. Perform a TWO-PASS review:\n\n   **Pass 1 — Understand intent:**\n   What is this branch trying to accomplish? Use the commit messages, diff, and the session context above.\n\n   **Pass 2 — Adversarial attack:**\n   Now assume the code is WRONG. For each changed file, ask:\n   - Can this crash? Under what input?\n   - Can this leak resources?\n   - Can this behave differently than intended?\n   - Does this handle errors properly?\n   - Is there a race condition or ordering issue?\n   - Does this break existing behavior?\n   - Does the implementation MATCH the stated intent from the session context?\n\n5. Output your findings in this format:\n\n   ## Branch Summary\n   One paragraph: what this branch does.\n\n   ## Findings\n\n   🔴 **CRITICAL** — [title]\n   - File: `path/to/file.py` (lines X-Y)\n   - Issue: what's wrong\n   - Scenario: when/how it fails\n   - Fix: suggested fix\n\n   🟡 **WARNING** — [title]\n   - File: ...\n   - Issue: ...\n   - Scenario: ...\n   - Fix: ...\n\n   🔵 **NOTICE** — [title]\n   - ...\n\n   ✅ **GOOD** — brief note on things done well (max 2-3)\n\n   ## Verdict\n   One sentence: is this branch ready to merge, or does it need fixes?\n\n6. Be HONEST. If the code is fine, say it's fine. Don't invent problems to look thorough. But if you find real issues, don't soften them.\n```\n\n### Step 4 — Present results\n\nWhen the sub-agent returns, present its findings to the user as-is. Don't editorialize or add fluff. If the sub-agent found critical issues, highlight them.\n\nIf the sub-agent failed (e.g., script error, no branch detected), relay the error clearly."
  }
}
